# Merging is a fold, so three failures answer three reasons however they were
# grouped. The merge conses a pair at each step unless it knows which errs
# were born merged, and then the same three reasons print three ways depending
# on where the parentheses fell.
#
# The bind is the other half: it is ordered, so the first failure is the
# answer and what follows never speaks. Both rules are here because the sweep
# that gave every operator the merge once gave it to sequencing too, on one
# engine only, and nothing said so.
#
# The reasons are read through `std/testing`'s `when_failed`: foreign to this
# module, so it is licensed to separate a reason from a failure. A reason
# that is itself a list can only be spelled, and a spelled err no longer
# reaches an operator, so the case that kept such a reason whole is gone.
#
# A raised err handed to an operator or to a group with no arm for it does
# not compile, bound to a name or not (#1884), so the failures here are the
# runtime's: `text/to_int` refusing text it cannot read. What the runtime does
# with a failure the checker was not warned about is what these lines pin.
import "std/testing"
import "std/text"

fn boom what
  text/to_int what

fn as_text reason
  "{reason}"

a = boom "a"

b = boom "b"

c = boom "c"

left = a + b + c

right = a + (b + c)

walled = a .> (_ -> b)

pub play = print "left first: {testing/when_failed left as_text}"
  .> (_ -> print "right first: {testing/when_failed right as_text}")
  .> (_ -> print "the wall: {testing/when_failed walled as_text}")
